{-
   A loop that counts and does nothing else, decided false at its header -
   `compiler/resolve_ssa/opt/loop.cpp`, and the seven shapes that must keep theirs. Both loop
   spellings are here: a `while` over storage the promotion turns into a counter, and a counted
   `for`, whose header the desugaring also hands its bound and its step - see phase 14.

   Every claim here is a **number**. A loop removed that should have run answers with the value its
   accumulator started at, and a loop kept that should have gone is invisible in an answer - so each
   function below is weighted by a distinct prime in `main` and is called with arguments that make
   the loop run more than zero times. `.run.expect` and the optimized/unoptimized comparison the
   driver runs beside it are what turn a wrong removal into a failure; `.ssa.opt.expect` says which
   loops are actually still there.

   The loop this pass exists for is not in this file and cannot be: it is `Reclaim(Array(a))` in
   `lib/Core/Array.yana`, one traversal over the live elements of an array whose element type
   has nothing to run - and a library body is left out of both dumps. `LiteralReclaim.yana`'s
   lowered golden is where it shows.

   The last two functions are not about loops at all. They are the two shapes phase 8 built the
   branch-implied facts for, measured, and did not keep - see `opt/README.md`'s note. They are here
   because what they record is a *structural* fact about this tier that a later change could undo:
   both need one SSA value to survive a block boundary, and at this tier every named value round
   trips through a slot, so the second test reads a second `load`. If cross-block promotion ever
   arrives here, this file's golden is what says so.
-}

data Held {id: Int}

let &live = 0 :: Int

instance Drop(Held):
    fn drop(->self: Held):
        live = live - 1

fn held(id: Int) -> Held:
    live = live + 1
    return Held {id: id}

-- The plain case. The body computes a value nothing reads, out of a local the loop only reads and a
-- counter nothing outside it reads, so the whole loop goes and `k` is the answer.
fn counted(n: Int, k: Int) -> Int:
    let &i = 0

    while i < n:
        let unused = k * i + 7
        i = i + 1

    return k

-- The body accumulates. `total` is a second value the loop carries round and the block after it
-- reads, which condition 3 refuses - so this loop stays and its answer is the sum.
fn accumulates(n: Int) -> Int:
    let &total = 0
    let &i = 0

    while i < n:
        total = total + i
        i = i + 1

    return total

-- The counter is read afterwards. Removing the loop answers the header's argument from the entry
-- edge, leaving `i` at what it started at rather than at the bound, which is why the pass asks about
-- the *block arguments* and not only about the values the loop's instructions define.
fn survives(n: Int) -> Int:
    let &i = 0

    while i < n:
        i = i + 1

    return i

{-
   A tracked value in the body.

   `held` is a call, so the purity clause refuses this loop before the ownership clause is reached -
   which is the same meeting point `IfConvert.yana`'s `chosen` records for if-conversion, and it is
   stated here rather than left to be discovered. Every way this stage produces an owner is a `call`
   or an `aggregate`, and the drop that ends one is not pure either, so the ownership clause has no
   case the purity clause has not already taken. It is here because "needs no rule of its own" and
   "never arrives" are different statements.
-}
fn owning(n: Int) -> Int:
    let &i = 0

    while i < n:
        let doomed = held(i)
        i = i + 1

    return 0

-- Two ways out. A `break` gives the loop a second exit edge, so "the header's test is false" stops
-- being a complete description of not running it and the pass declines.
fn escapes(n: Int, stop: Int) -> Int:
    let &i = 0

    while i < n:
        if i == stop then break
        i = i + 1

    return i

{-
   The same three shapes written as a counted `for`, which is phase 14.

   Phase 13's review found every loop this pass fires on was a `while`, and the `for` form not
   reachable at all: the desugaring hands the header its bound and its step as block arguments of
   their own, `bb1(%bound, %step, %counter)`, and the latch answers each of them with what the header
   gave it. So `usedOutside` declined on the *bound* leaving over the exit edge before the counter
   was looked at, and `stepsByOne` saw the step as a block argument rather than as a `Const`.
   `removeTrivialArguments` is what removes both, and these three are what says so: `deadFor` loses
   its loop, and the two below keep theirs for the two reasons a `while` keeps one.
-}
fn deadFor(n: Int, k: Int) -> Int:
    for i in 0..n:
        let unused = k * i + 7

    return k

-- The `for` body accumulates, which is `accumulates` one shape up: `total` is carried round the
-- header and read by the block after it, so condition 3 refuses the loop.
fn forAccumulates(n: Int) -> Int:
    let &total = 0

    for i in 0..n:
        total = total + i

    return total

{-
   A `for` the counter escapes from - `escapes` written the other way.

   A `for`'s own `i` is not in scope below the loop, so what carries the count out is a binding the
   body writes; the `break` is what makes it worth carrying, and it is also the second exit edge that
   ends the pass's "the header's test is false" description of not running the loop. Both halves have
   to hold for this to be a fixture rather than a duplicate of the one above.
-}
fn forBreaks(n: Int, stop: Int) -> Int:
    let &at = 0

    for i in 0..n:
        if i == stop:
            at = i
            break

    return at

-- Nested, with the inner one dead and the outer one not. The inner goes on the first round and the
-- outer keeps its own step, which is what the innermost-first order buys.
fn nested(rows: Int, columns: Int) -> Int:
    let &total = 0
    let &row = 0

    while row < rows:
        let &column = 0
        while column < columns:
            let unused = column + row
            column = column + 1

        total = total + row
        row = row + 1

    return total

{-
   And the other loop question in that file: a loop the entry edge's own numbers say is never
   entered - `unenteredLoops`, which proves the opposite thing to everything above it.

   Every refusal this file has recorded so far is a refusal about what the body *does*, and none of
   them applies here: the three below call, write a global, carry a value out and leave by two
   different exits, and the one that goes, goes anyway. What is being proved is that none of it
   happens, so what the body is made of is not a question.

   `visits` is what makes that a number rather than a claim about the IR. A loop wrongly removed
   leaves it at zero and a loop wrongly kept raises it, and `main` weights it like everything else -
   so the `.run.expect` and the optimized-against-unoptimized comparison catch a fold in either
   direction, which the answers alone would not: `neverRuns` returns the same thing whether or not
   the pass fires, and that is exactly the point of it.
-}

let &visits = 0 :: Int

{-
   The shape, with every refusal above it present at once.

   `i` starts at ten against a bound of four, so the header's test is false the first time it is
   asked and the induction does the rest. The body writes a global, the `break` is a second way out,
   and `i` is read below the loop - which are conditions 2, 1 and 3 of the pass above, all three
   failing. The loop still goes.
-}
fn neverRuns(stop: Int) -> Int:
    let &i = 10
    let &total = 0

    while i < 4:
        visits = visits + 1
        total = total + i
        if i == stop then break
        i = i + 1

    return total + i

{-
   The mirror, and it is the reason the pass asks which arm the answer selects rather than only
   whether there is one.

   The test holds on the way in, which says the body runs once and nothing more - the second visit is
   decided by what the latch hands over, and that is not a number anything here holds. So the branch
   keeps its condition and the loop keeps its body; `visits` counts the turns, and the answer is the
   sum the loop accumulated rather than the one it started with.
-}
fn entersOnce(stop: Int) -> Int:
    let &i = 0
    let &total = 0

    while i < 4:
        visits = visits + 1
        total = total + i
        if i == stop then break
        i = i + 1

    return total + i

-- And the entry operand that is not a number at all. The header's argument arrives from a parameter,
-- so there is nothing to compute the test over and the loop stays whatever the bound is.
fn unknownStart(from: Int) -> Int:
    let &i = from
    let &total = 0

    while i < 4:
        visits = visits + 1
        total = total + i
        i = i + 1

    return total + i

{-
   The first shape phase 8 measured and did not keep: a zero test a dominating branch already
   decided.

   `d != 0` guards the division and `d == 0` inside the arm asks the same question again, which the
   shipping stage's `foldProvenZeroTests` answered outright. Here the two tests are not tests of one
   value: `d` lives in a slot, `forwardMemory` is a per-block scan, and the guard's block and the
   arm's block therefore each hold their own `load`. Measured over the whole corpus, the walk asked
   627 times and never once found a second zero test of the same SSA value in one body.

   Division needs no guard in this language - `doc/spec/types.md` makes it total and
   `makeDivisionTotal` at the lower tier implements it - so this shape is one a program has to be
   written to contain, which is the other half of why there is no pass for it.
-}
fn guarded(x: Int, d: Int) -> Int:
    if d != 0:
        let quotient = x / d
        if d == 0 then return -1
        return quotient

    return 0

{-
   The second: the sign/zero extension pair a checked subscript emits over there, which no subscript
   emits here.

   An index at this tier is a `Size` handed straight to `element.addr`, with the stride multiply the
   lowering's, and the bounds test `lib/Core/Array.yana` writes is a `bitcast` to `USize` rather
   than a widening conversion - so a subscript produces no extension at all. Written out by hand, as
   below, the two extensions are still not of one value, because the unsigned one goes through a
   `bitcast` first. Measured over the whole corpus: no value is both sign-extended and zero-extended
   anywhere.
-}
fn widened(i: Int, n: UInt) -> Long:
    if (bitcast(i) :: UInt) < n:
        let wide = i :: Long
        let unsignedWide = (bitcast(i) :: UInt) :: ULong
        return wide + (bitcast(unsignedWide) :: Long)

    return 0

fn main() -> Int =
    counted(4, 3) * 2 + accumulates(5) * 3 + survives(6) * 5 + owning(4) * 7 +
    escapes(9, 4) * 11 + nested(3, 4) * 13 + guarded(84, 4) * 17 +
    (truncate(widened(6, 9)) :: Int) * 19 + live * 23 +
    deadFor(4, 3) * 29 + forAccumulates(5) * 31 + forBreaks(9, 4) * 37 +
    neverRuns(2) * 41 + entersOnce(2) * 43 + unknownStart(1) * 47 + visits * 53
